Read the real LLM-generated world_model.py for sp80, cd82, and g50t
directly (real code from this session's own live induction attempts),
rather than continuing to reason from aggregate trust metrics. This
refutes the leading hypothesis (an execution/plumbing bug, motivated by
the suspiciously uniform zero-everywhere failure pattern) -- the code is
syntactically valid and non-crashing in every case.

Two precise, source-verified root causes instead:

1. _transitions_block's uncapped-default k=8 (arc_executable_world_model.py
   ~line 1054) shows the LLM at most 6 grid-changing transitions of the 25
   collected -- roughly one per action type, the exact data-starvation
   signature that produces g50t's memorize-the-literal-coordinate engine
   (grid[63, 62] = 1 hardcoded per action, despite the prompt explicitly
   instructing "prefer simple general rules").

2. The win-state block requires either a level-up transition (impossible
   on a first-contact level by construction) or _previous_level_complete_
   grid (REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-18: None until a level completes once) -- so
   first-contact levels supply the LLM zero positive win-state information.
   cd82's is_level_complete: return False is close to the epistemically
   honest answer given no positive evidence. This is the SAME upstream
   first-contact data gap REQ-ARC-FCP-5699-18 found crippling tier 1's
   goal-energy, now shown to independently starve tier 2's goal-predicate
   induction too.

Scope limit: n=3 games' code read directly; the k=8 fix is source-verified
plausible but not yet empirically tested.

